  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender-Inclusive Education: Preventing Gender-Based Violence, Building a Fairer &amp; Safer Society through Schools</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Contract related to: Gender-Inclusive Education: Preventing Gender-Based Violence, Building a Fairer &amp; Safer Society through Schools - This project, as the first and only G2 action under the 2021-2027 South Africa MIP, aims to address gender-based violence (GBV) in and around schools across four key provinces in South Africa, building on previous successful initiatives conducted by NGOs, UN Agencies and Member States while aligning with broader actions under the Annual Action Plan 2025 (AAP2025). Specifically, the action would build on the work carried out under previous EU funded actions focusing on addressing the lack of accountability for GBV, more closely concentrating on young people in local communities and the education sector. The overarching goal is to reduce inequalities and create a conducive environment to equip women and girls with the skills and opportunities needed to access key sectors within the EU-South Africa partnership, including strategic value chains like critical raw materials (CRM) and green hydrogen. Central to this effort is the education system, which plays a critical role in preparing women and girls for emerging jobs in these sectors. The project will focus geographically on the North-South corridor, which includes the provinces of KwaZulu-Natal, Gauteng, Mpumalanga, and Limpopo. This will ensure a coherent 360&#xb0; approach with other EU-SA initiatives which primarily focus on creation of enabling environments for a just energy transition in the country, minimising environmental and social impact while promoting access to benefits for youth, women and disadvantaged communities. It is therefore essential to ensure that a community-based and human rights-based approach is pursued to tackle the root-causes of GBV to compliment the effective implementation of such EU-SA initiatives as the issues differ so substantially between grassroots communities in these regions. Through a three-pillar approach of capacity-building, school community-led campaigns, and private sector outreach, the project will promote education on gender equality and respect for pupils, teachers, and parents, creating safer school environments and challenging harmful gender norms. This is expected to significantly reduce violence and discrimination in both schools and the wider community.</narrative>
  </description>
